  {
   "slug": "french-press-without-the-sludge",
   "title": "French Press Without the Sludge",
   "date": "2026-10-02T09:30:00",
   "excerpt": "A rich, full-bodied cup with none of the grit at the bottom. The trick is patience, not plunging.",
   "categories": [
    "brewing-guides"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "immersion",
    "at-home"
   ],
   "featured_media": "frenchpress",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The French press is the most forgiving brewer there is, and the most misunderstood. Most muddy cups come from plunging too hard and too soon.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Our method lets gravity do the filtering. It takes a few extra minutes but gives you a clean cup with all of the body you love.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"brew-specs\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group brew-specs\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Clean press method</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"recipe-facts\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list recipe-facts\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Coffee</strong>30 g</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Water</strong>500 g</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Grind</strong>Medium</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Temp</strong>95&deg;C</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Time</strong>9 min</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"className\":\"steps\"} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list steps\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Add coffee and all the water; do not stir yet.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>After 4 minutes, stir the crust and scoop off the foam.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Wait another 5 minutes while the grounds sink.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Press the plunger just to the surface; do not push down.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pour slowly, leaving the last bit in the pot.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:frenchpress}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:frenchpress}}\" alt=\"A French press next to a cup of coffee\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:frenchpress}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Try it with one of our chocolatey blends. The longer steep brings out a lovely sweetness.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "the-aeropress-three-ways",
   "title": "The AeroPress, Three Ways",
   "date": "2026-09-15T10:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Strong and short, bright and clean, or iced over a glass: three recipes from our team for the little brewer that does everything.",
   "categories": [
    "brewing-guides"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "immersion",
    "at-home"
   ],
   "featured_media": "aeropress",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every barista on our team has a favourite AeroPress recipe, and they will argue about it for hours. Here are three that settled the debate, at least for a week.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>All three use the inverted method, where the brewer starts upside down so nothing drips through early.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Short &amp; strong</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>18 g coffee, fine grind, 90 g water at 90&deg;C. Stir, steep 1:30, press. Top up with milk for a quick flat white.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Bright &amp; clean</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>12 g coffee, medium grind, 200 g water at 96&deg;C. Steep 2:00, two papers, press slowly.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Iced</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>16 g coffee, fine grind, 100 g hot water. Steep 1:00, press directly over 100 g of ice.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:aeropress}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:aeropress}}\" alt=\"An AeroPress brewer with a mug and coffee beans\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:aeropress}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:pullquote --><figure class=\"wp-block-pullquote\"><blockquote><p>Small brewer, big opinions.</p></blockquote></figure><!-- /wp:pullquote -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Felix",
     "content": "The iced one is my summer go-to now.",
     "date": "2026-09-17T14:20:00",
     "email": "felix@example.com"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "meet-our-washed-ethiopian",
   "title": "Meet Our New Washed Ethiopian",
   "date": "2026-10-12T08:45:00",
   "excerpt": "Jasmine, bergamot and ripe peach. The story of the new coffee on our filter bar, from the hills to your cup.",
   "categories": [
    "origins"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "filter",
    "single-origin"
   ],
   "featured_media": "origin",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This season&rsquo;s washed Ethiopian arrived last week, and the whole team has been brewing it non-stop. It is floral, juicy and incredibly clean.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The coffee comes from a cooperative of small farms at high altitude, where cherries ripen slowly in cool air. Slower ripening means more sugar in the bean and a sweeter cup.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Tasting notes</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Jasmine and bergamot on the nose</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Ripe peach and honey in the cup</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A long, tea-like finish</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:origin}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:origin}}\" alt=\"Coffee cherries and green beans on a table\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:origin}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We roast it light to keep those delicate flavours. It shines as a pour-over and is surprisingly lovely as a cold brew.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A coffee you can smell from across the room.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Our roaster</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "cold-brew-concentrate-at-home",
   "title": "Cold Brew Concentrate at Home",
   "date": "2026-07-08T11:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Make a jar on Sunday and have smooth iced coffee all week. One ratio, one jar and a night in the fridge.",
   "categories": [
    "drinks-at-home"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "cold-brew",
    "at-home"
   ],
   "featured_media": "coldbrew",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Cold brew is the easiest coffee you will ever make: grounds, cold water and time. Because it is never heated, it is low in bitterness and naturally sweet.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We make it as a concentrate, then dilute it with water or milk. That way a single jar lasts the whole week.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"brew-specs\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group brew-specs\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Cold brew concentrate</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"recipe-facts\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list recipe-facts\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Coffee</strong>100 g</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Water</strong>500 g</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Grind</strong>Coarse</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Steep</strong>16 hr</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Keeps</strong>7 days</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"className\":\"steps\"} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list steps\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Stir coarse coffee into cold water in a large jar.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cover and steep in the fridge for 14&ndash;18 hours.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Strain through a sieve lined with a paper filter.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Dilute one part concentrate to two parts water or milk.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Serve over plenty of ice.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:coldbrew}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:coldbrew}}\" alt=\"A jar of cold brew with ice and a glass\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:coldbrew}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Fancy something sweeter? Stir a spoonful of maple syrup into the concentrate before diluting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Nadia",
     "content": "Made this with your house blend and it is dangerously good.",
     "date": "2026-07-10T16:00:00",
     "email": "nadia@example.com"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "iced-mint-mocha",
   "title": "Our Iced Mint Mocha",
   "date": "2026-08-02T15:00:00",
   "excerpt": "The summer special everyone asks about: dark chocolate, fresh mint syrup and a double shot over ice.",
   "categories": [
    "drinks-at-home"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "cold-brew",
    "seasonal"
   ],
   "featured_media": "mocha",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every summer we put one drink on the board just for fun. This year it was the iced mint mocha, and it outsold everything except the flat white.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The secret is real mint: we steep fresh leaves in sugar syrup overnight instead of using flavouring.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Fresh mint syrup</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Heat 200 g sugar with 200 ml water until dissolved.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Take off the heat and add a big handful of mint.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Steep overnight, then strain and refrigerate.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:mocha}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:mocha}}\" alt=\"A tall iced mocha with mint leaves\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:mocha}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To build the drink: a spoon of chocolate sauce, a spoon of mint syrup, a double espresso, ice and cold milk. Stir and garnish with a mint sprig.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "comments": []
  },
  {
   "slug": "our-new-roastery-corner",
   "title": "Our New Roastery Corner",
   "date": "2026-09-01T09:00:00",
   "excerpt": "We now roast in small batches right behind the counter. Come and watch on Tuesday and Thursday mornings.",
   "categories": [
    "cafe-news"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "roasting"
   ],
   "featured_media": "roastery",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>After two years of planning, a small drum roaster has moved into the corner of our Market Street shop. Every bag on our shelves is now roasted a few metres from where you order.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Roasting in small batches lets us try new coffees quickly and keep everything fresh. Most beans go from roaster to shelf within three days.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Roasting mornings</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Time</th><th>What we roast</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>8:00&ndash;11:00</td><td>Espresso blends</td></tr><tr><td>Thursday</td><td>8:00&ndash;11:00</td><td>Single origins</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:roastery}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:roastery}}\" alt=\"A small coffee roaster with sacks of green beans\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:roastery}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Pull up a stool and ask questions. Our roaster loves to talk about first crack.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Ollie",
     "content": "Watched the roast on Tuesday, the smell is unreal.",
     "date": "2026-09-03T12:30:00",
     "email": "ollie@example.com"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "latte-art-basics",
   "title": "Latte Art Basics: Your First Heart",
   "date": "2026-08-20T10:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Silky milk and a steady pour. Our training notes for drawing a heart at home with any steam wand.",
   "categories": [
    "brewing-guides"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "espresso",
    "at-home"
   ],
   "featured_media": "latteart",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every new barista on our team spends their first week pouring hearts. It is the foundation of every other pattern, and it is all about milk texture.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You want milk like wet paint: glossy, smooth and without visible bubbles. Stretch it briefly at the start, then let it spin.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"brew-specs\",\"layout\":{\"type\":\"constrained\"}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group brew-specs\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Pouring a heart</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"recipe-facts\"} --><ul class=\"wp-block-list recipe-facts\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Milk</strong>150 ml</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Temp</strong>60&deg;C</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Stretch</strong>3 sec</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><strong>Cup</strong>200 ml</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:list {\"ordered\":true,\"className\":\"steps\"} --><ol class=\"wp-block-list steps\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pour from high into the centre to mix the crema.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>When the cup is half full, bring the jug close.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pour faster so a white circle appears.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Lift slightly and cut through the circle to finish.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ol><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:group -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:latteart}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:latteart}}\" alt=\"A latte with a heart pattern in the foam\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:latteart}}\"/></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:quote --><blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Wobbly hearts still taste great. Keep pouring.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><cite>Training notes</cite></blockquote><!-- /wp:quote -->",
   "comments": []
  }
 ],
